<title>UK: IGC cuts 1996/97 China wheat imports to 9.0 mln T.</title>
<headline>IGC cuts 1996/97 China wheat imports to 9.0 mln T.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-08-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Improving wheat yields in China this year should trim its import needs to 9.0 million tonnes in 1996/97 from an estimated 12.7 million in the previous season, the International Grains Council (IGC) said on Friday.</p>
<p>In its latest monthly report, the IGC cut its forecast for Chinese wheat imports from an earlier estimate of 11.5 million tonnes.</p>
<p>Wheat output in 1996 was projected at 106 million tonnes, up from last month's 104 million and compared with 103 million tonnes in 1995 following reports of better yields and favourable weather.</p>
<p>China is the world's biggest buyer of wheat.</p>
<p>The IGC put wheat shipments to China since July at 2.5 million tonnes, mainly from Australia and Canada. --Clelia Oziel, London Newsroom +44 171 542 8072</p>
</text>
